          "case_name": "Cruz v. Jackson County Department of Human Services",
          "citation": "133 So. 3d 335 (Miss. 2013)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of Mississippi",
          "year": 2013,
          "date_decided": "2013-05-23",
          "holding": "The Court affirmed the youth court's order keeping its records confidential but reversed and rendered as to a gag order barring participants from discussing the case, finding no compelling state interest supported it.",
          "relevance": "Distinguishes lawful record confidentiality from an unlawful prior restraint on the parties themselves.",
          "quote": "We find that the youth court did not abuse its discretion in ordering that its records remain confidential.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/5105283/cruz-v-jackson-county-department-of-human-services/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 5105283
        },
        {
          "case_name": "M.D.H. v. State",
          "citation": "726 So. 2d 547 (Miss. 1998)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of Mississippi",
          "year": 1998,
          "date_decided": "1998-03-26",
          "holding": "The Court read Section 43-21-261(1) broadly and held that a criminal defendant was entitled to limited, court-supervised access to youth court records and the transcript concerning his accuser.",
          "relevance": "Confidentiality is not absolute; a court may order limited disclosure where the statute's own exceptions are engaged.",
          "quote": "the best interest of the child, as well the proper and just functioning of our youth court system, demands that Michael have limited access to the materials he seeks",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/7781017/mdh-v-state/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 7781017
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Pass Christian School District v. Harrison County Youth Court of Mississippi",
          "citation": "275 So. 3d 1096 (Miss. 2019)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of Mississippi",
          "year": 2019,
          "date_decided": "2019-07-25",
          "holding": "The Court held that youth court records and transcripts could not be released directly to a civil defendant, but reversed in part and remanded so the record could be reviewed in camera to determine what, if anything, should be disclosed.",
          "relevance": "Sets the procedure - in camera review rather than outright refusal - when an agency or court withholds youth court material from a litigant.",
          "quote": "Disclosure of youth-court records is subject to the discretion of the youth-court judge.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4642938/pass-christian-school-district-v-harrison-county-youth-court-of/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 4642938
        },
        {
          "case_name": "State v. U.G.",
          "citation": "726 So. 2d 151 (Miss. 1998)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of Mississippi",
          "year": 1998,
          "date_decided": "1998-10-29",
          "holding": "The Court reversed and remanded a circuit court order transferring an indicted juvenile to youth court, holding among other things that the judge abused his discretion by obtaining the youth court and school records for in camera inspection while denying both parties any access to them.",
          "relevance": "Withholding youth court records entirely from the parties, instead of fixing the extent and purpose of disclosure, is an abuse of discretion and defeats appellate review of the decision that relied on them.",
          "quote": "The judge does have discretion to determine the extent and purpose of disclosure. However, he abused his discretion in this case by keeping all records from the parties",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/1778690/state-v-ug/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 1778690
        }
